@@ -214,6 +214,10 @@ The log file gains a `(links <bin dir>)` line after `(cwd …)`. Background comm
 
 `forge job assist <id> --gate <class>` is read-only and returns `{gate, stale, reserved, model, effort, subagent, brief}`: the `forge:assistant` spawn for that gate, with a brief of paths only. `stale: true` means the job no longer waits on that gate, and `reserved` is re-checked at call time. An unknown `--gate` exits 1, and so does a daemon session other than the office. `forge requests add gate` always exits 1: only `forge job set` and `forge pr sync` write gate requests.
 
+## Opening a PR
+
+`forge pr open <id> --dry-run` runs every check `forge pr open` runs before it pushes (an unpromoted downstream repo, the worktree, a verified HEAD unless `--force`, the secret scan of the PR title, body and commits after refreshing the base from the push remote, protected paths, at least one commit ahead of the base, and the interim strategies) and refuses exactly as it would, with the same code and exit. When every check passes it prints the plan instead of pushing: `{ dryRun: true, title, body, base, head, draft, commit, remote, slug, pr, push, labels, reviewers, repos?, deferred? }`. `head` is the job branch, `commit` the checked commit the push would send (rewritten first when a message carries AI attribution), `slug` the GitHub `owner/name` from `tracker.github.repo` or the remote url (null when neither names one), `pr` the PR number the job already records, and `push` false when the root PR already merged or an interim strategy holds it back. A job with member repos lists each one the open would push under `repos` (`{ repo, slug, base, head, commit, draft, title, body }`). A dry run never pushes, never calls `gh` (so it needs no `gh` installed, and an interim merge order is judged on the PR states the job records) and never changes the job: `job.json` and `events.jsonl` stay as they were, and a refusal logs nothing.
+
 ## Merging
 
 `forge pr merge <id> [--repo NAME | --root] [--now]` syncs first, then merges one PR at a time with exactly one `gh pr merge` call each: directly when checks pass or the repo has no CI, queued with `--auto` while checks are pending only when GitHub holds the PR and no protected-paths policy applies, deferred otherwise, and refused when checks fail. A single-repo job has one PR, and the result is `{ merged, autoMerge, action, checks, mergeState?, error? }` with `action` one of `already-merged`, `merged`, `auto`, `deferred`, `refused`, `failed`.
